They're not bad.  (Don't forget, Kernighan was Stroustrup's boss
when Stroustrup wrote those books.)  But for the shear quality
of the writing, Kernighan can't be beaten.

> But IMHO nobody should learn C++ before they have learned OO.

It depends.  I find OO a very powerful idiom, and use it a lot.
But you can do a lot in C++ without it as well.  And having all
the necessary data structures already implemented, and not
having to deal with pointers and pointer arithmetic just because
you need an array, make C++ easier thna C as a procedural
language as well.

